{% extends 'guide/layout.html.twig' %}

{% block guide_content %}
<p class="guide-lead">A baby prediction game, sometimes called a baby pool, is a game where family and friends bet on the baby's arrival: date, time, weight, length, gender, name. It has long been played among relatives and colleagues on a notebook, a poster or, more and more, online. Here is everything you need to run one smoothly.</p>

<h2>The most popular questions</h2>
<p>Every category has its own suspense. A few reference points to steer your own predictions:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>The birth date</strong> is the most contested. Only about 5% of babies are born on their due date, so there is always something to debate, before and after the day.</li>
    <li><strong>Weight</strong>: most babies weigh between 2.5 and 4 kg (5.5 to 8.8 lb) at birth, with an average around 3.3 kg (about 7 lb 4 oz).</li>
    <li><strong>Length</strong> is around 50 cm (20 inches), most often between 48 and 52 cm.</li>
    <li><strong>Gender</strong>: a fifty-fifty call, but everyone has their instincts, signs and superstitions.</li>
    <li><strong>The name</strong> is the hardest to guess: "hints mode", which gives three guided tries, makes it easier and more fun.</li>
</ul>
<p>Keep it to three to five questions: beyond that, guests lose steam.</p>

<h2>Clear rules to pick winners</h2>
<p>Settle the rules before opening the game, to avoid any argument at the reveal:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Gender and name</strong>: everyone who guessed right wins.</li>
    <li><strong>Date, weight, length</strong>: the closest prediction wins. Keep the three closest to build a podium.</li>
    <li><strong>Ties</strong>: the first person to answer wins, or a draw. Your call, as long as you say so in advance.</li>
    <li><strong>Overall champion</strong>: one point per category won, and the total crowns the family champion.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Prize ideas (without breaking the bank)</h2>
<ul>
    <li>A small plant or a bouquet.</li>
    <li>A children's book the baby will soon be reading.</li>
    <li>A basket of treats.</li>
    <li>A home-cooked dinner from the parents-to-be.</li>
    <li>An honorary title such as "official family fortune teller".</li>
</ul>

<h2>Paper or online?</h2>
<p>Paper suits a small gathering. As soon as the family is spread out, an online game is more practical: one link to share, guests who answer from their phone without creating an account, and a ranking calculated automatically. Guests who want to be told about the reveal can leave their email address. We cover the setup in our guide to the <a href="{{ path('app_guide', {'_locale': 'en', 'slug': 'baby-shower-guessing-game'}) }}">baby shower guessing game</a>.</p>

<h2>Mistakes to avoid</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Too many questions</strong>: you lose half your players along the way.</li>
    <li><strong>Opening predictions too late</strong>: launch the game as soon as the pregnancy is announced, or at least a few weeks before the due date.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting to close it</strong>: pick an end date and close the game at that moment.</li>
    <li><strong>Changing the rules afterwards</strong>: the surest way to upset a grandfather who is confident in his guess.</li>
</ul>
